A||||4||Old Lar||88 oil sending unit||||5||
Z||000000||Old Lar||05-16-2001||08:37 PM||||My oil guage is pegged when engine is running.  Previously on my 87 it is the sending unit.<P>It looks like such a simple fix.  I got a replacement sending unit, I unplug the wire plug to the old.  I bought a 1 1/16 wrench to take the old one out, but there is no room to work.  Unless I remove the battery and shelf or drop the engine.  Deep sockets won't go over the oblong oil electrical connector.  I got special stubby wrenches for the 87 oil sending unit but this one has the big hex nut on it.  The stubby wrenches are not made (by Sears) at 1 1/16 inch size.<P>What a pain it the butt.<P>I'm ready to take it back to Mr Goodwrench and ask them to unsrew the old and screw in the new because this is screwy.<P>Any suggestions?||||reg||5
Z||000001||PJ||05-16-2001||10:03 PM||||Maybe a oxygen sensor socket would fit. Or a crowfoot with an extension. If not you could pull the whole bracket and tubing and then remove it. The '88's had a service bulletin about relocating the sender next to the oil filter. Let me know if you need a copy of it.<P>PJ<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||05-16-2001||10:11 PM||||there are several differant oil pressure sender sockets, one for short senders. one for long senders and than there is an older mid 80 socket.  Suggest go to GM dealer and maybe they would loan it.  No harm in asking.  I have folks asking for tools/advise at work all the time.  It pays to help others.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||05-17-2001||07:37 AM||||There should be a 6 point nut type (13mm or 15mm I think) at the very top of the nipple under the main body of the unit. Make sure to have a good hold of the mounting block of the unit, no need to twist the line.<BR>You may still need to remove the battery to gain access.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||05-17-2001||07:42 AM||||Thanks for the input..No time left if I want a working guage for the Carlisle trip, so I dropped it off at Mr. Goodwrench this am.<P>Ed the technician will love it.  I said its a 10 minute job if you got the right tool.  How hard is it to srew in a light bulb?<P>Rodney will need to make a special tool for old pressure sending unit removal.<P>Mr. Goodwrench had the right tool, a real deep 1 1/16 inch socket that would fit over the sending unit and unscrew it, replace with a new one. Only 1/2 hour labor charge.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||9
